# Non-SGML characters (BEL, 7) in the three MARKUP contexts onsgmls checks with
# reportNonSgmlCharacter (parseParam / doDeclSubset / parseAttributeValueLiteral),
# complementing synth-non-sgml-char (which covers content data):
#   - inside a model group:  <!ELEMENT b - - (x&#7;)>   (parse_param/group)
#   - between declarations:   a bare BEL on its own line (parse_decl_subset)
#   - in an attribute value:  m="p&#7;q"                (parse_literal)
# Our scanner admits C0/DEL as sgmlChar (they stay data in content), so these
# reach the tokenChar / tokenUnrecognized / recover arms rather than the
# reference's tokenUnrecognized; each is reported and DROPPED (attribute value
# "pq", not "p<BEL>q"), and the parse recovers to the same ESIS. Exit 1.
# (Non-SGML inside the SGML DECLARATION itself is not covered: the scoped
# SdParser is a byte-level keyword tokenizer that never routes through the
# diagnostic infrastructure; corpus declarations are clean.)
BASE=entry
DOC=doc.sgm
